3. Walking and oxygen model (plan B, labelled ESTIMATE on screen)
==========================================================================

Every walking and oxygen number in the app is labelled:
  "ESTIMATE: plan B over the relief, not the walking model yet"

Constants used (Assets/Walkback/App/ReturnStandIn.cs, HudEstimate.cs,
TerrainLayers.cs, ReturnFallback.cs). "Same figure as" and "for
comparison" mean a published document gives that number; they do not
mean the document validates this prototype. The NASA documents below
were read on 15 Nov 2026.

  Slope pace           Tobler's hiking function,
                       W = 6 exp(-3.5 |S + 0.05|) km/h, S = rise / run.
                       Tobler, W. (1993), "Three presentations on
                       geographical analysis and modeling", NCGIA
                       Technical Report 93-1 (cited in ReturnFallback.cs).
                       Measured on Earth, without a suit. The app keeps
                       only the shape of the curve (1 on flat ground);
                       its flat-ground pace is the next line, not
                       Tobler's 5 km/h.
  Flat-ground pace     19.3 min/km (3.1 km/h). From the team's model
                       notes ("ficha A"); no published source for it is
                       written in this repository. For comparison,
                       NASA's Exploration EVA concept of operations
                       assumes about 2 km/h for walking traverses on the
                       Moon, a value it marks as still to be resolved
                       (EVA-EXP-0042 Rev B, section 4.2.1.3.1.1).
  Slope limit          20 degrees, no step on steeper ground. Same figure
                       as the slope the Exploration EVA suit is to walk
                       up, down or across (EVA-EXP-0042 Rev B, section
                       4.2.1.3).
  Design duration      480 min (8 h) for the full tank. Same figure as
                       the longest EVA the Exploration EVA suit supports
                       (EVA-EXP-0042 Rev B, sections 4.1.4 and 4.2.1.1).
  Design rate          351 W, which is 1200 Btu/h: the average metabolic
                       rate at which the Apollo portable life support
                       system's duration is stated (4 h for the -6 unit,
                       6 h for the -7; its average metabolic load
                       requirement was 1600 Btu/h), and the average that
                       studies expected for a 4-hour lunar surface EVA
                       (Beggs, J. C. and Goodwin, F. H., "Apollo PLSS:
                       environmental control of the smallest manned
                       space vehicle", Hamilton Standard, 1972, NASA NTRS
                       19720019459, table 3.1). Eight hours at that rate
                       is this prototype's own combination.
  Tank                 497 L of O2. Derived, not published: 351 W for
                       480 min is about 10.1 MJ, which is 497 L at about
                       20.3 kJ per litre of oxygen. The conversion is
                       from the team's notes; its source is not written
                       in this repository.
  Reserve              31 L, never touched. Derived: 30 min at the design
                       rate (497 L / 480 min). For comparison, the
                       emergency oxygen of the Apollo OPS and of the
                       Shuttle suit's secondary oxygen pack lasts 30 min
                       (NTRS 19720019459; NASA EG-1998-03-112-HQ,
                       "Suited for Spacewalking"); the Exploration EVA
                       suit plans 1 hour (EVA-EXP-0042 Rev B, 4.2.1.1).
  Oxygen walking       20 L of O2 per km on flat ground. Derived:
                       19.3 min at the design rate.
  Task minutes         Proposed by the team. Miller, M. J., Claybrook,
                       A., Greenlund, S., Marquez, J. J. and Feigh, K. M.
                       (2017), "Operational Assessment of Apollo Lunar
                       Surface Extravehicular Activity",
                       NASA/TP-2017-219457, table 30, gives the Apollo
                       statistics for tasks at a station (median 9 to
                       11 min, quartiles 4 to 25 min); it does not give
                       times for these particular tasks.

  These are the prototype's settings, not validated figures for a suited
  astronaut on Mars: the pace and the oxygen numbers were not measured in
  a suit or in Mars gravity. The app labels every number made with them
  ESTIMATE.


