8. Version 1.1 (branch v1.1): the time at Gale and the SUITS link
==========================================================================

The time at Gale (Assets/Walkback/App/MarsClock.cs)
  The Mars24 algorithm of NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies:
  Allison, M. and McEwen, M. (2000), "A post-Pathfinder evaluation of
  areocentric solar coordinates with improved timing recipes for Mars
  seasonal/diurnal climate studies", Planetary and Space Science 48,
  215-235; https://www.giss.nasa.gov/tools/mars24/help/algorithm.html
  Written from the published steps (no code copied). The app uses it for
  the local mean solar time (LMST), the season (Ls), Curiosity's sol, and
  sunrise and sunset at the center of the Gale square (4.807 S,
  137.381 E). The automatic test checks it against five days of the
  Curiosity REMS weather feed (NASA/JPL-Caltech/CAB), sols 100 to 4995:
  the sol and the sunrise and sunset agree (within 1.2 min), Ls within
  1.5 degrees.

The NASA SUITS telemetry stream server (optional link, off by default)
  github.com/SUITS-Techteam (TSS2026, TSS2027): the request format (UDP,
  port 14141, command 1 = EVA.json, big-endian) and the field names read
  (oxy_pri_storage and others) were taken from their public README,
  EVA.json and server.c. No code of TSS is in this repository. TSS's
  values come from its own simulation; the app says so on the card.
